    "content": "Somali Interior Minister Abdullahi Sheikh Ismail (Fartaag) and the commander of the Somali National Army arrived in Kampala, Uganda's capital, on Tuesday, preceding a scheduled visit by President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud. The delegation is expected to participate in high-level discussions focused on the African Union's peacekeeping mission in Somalia.\n\nThe upcoming conference will assess the security situation in Somalia and evaluate the role of the African Union Transition Mission (ATMIS) as the country prepares for a potential transition of security responsibilities to Somali forces. These talks come at a critical juncture as Somalia continues to rebuild its security institutions after decades of conflict.\n\nPresident Mohamud is expected to join the meetings in the coming days, highlighting the importance the Somali government places on these negotiations. The discussions aim to shape the future structure and timeline of the AU mission, which has been instrumental in supporting Somali forces against militant groups.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are significant as they directly impact the stability and security landscape in Somalia. Many diaspora members maintain close ties with relatives in the country and follow political and security progress closely. A successful transition could pave the way for increased investment, development, and eventual returns for those considering repatriation.\n\nThe outcome of the Kampala talks will be closely watched by international partners and the Somali public, as it determines the extent of continued international support for Somalia's security sector in the coming years.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/28/wasiirka-amniga-iyo-taliyaha-ciidanka-xds-oo-u-ambabaxay-kampala-ka-hor-safarka-madaxweyne-xasan-sheekh/",
