    "content": "Former Somali Prime Minister Omar Abdirashid Ali Sharmarke has raised concerns over rising military tensions between the federal government and Puntland state near the strategic town of Galkayo. In a statement issued on Tuesday, Sharmarke warned that the ongoing buildup of forces in the area could spark a new internal conflict, which would divert critical resources away from the fight against militant groups such as Al-Shabaab and ISIS.\n\nGalkayo, a divided city in central Somalia, has long been a flashpoint between Puntland and the federal government due to contested territorial claims. The recent deployment of additional troops by both sides has heightened fears of armed confrontation, exacerbating an already fragile political climate. Sharmarke, who served as prime minister from 2014 to 2017, emphasized that such escalation would undermine security gains and deepen the country's political crisis.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, particularly those with family and business ties in central Somalia, the prospect of renewed conflict raises concerns about safety, displacement, and economic disruption. Many rely on Galkayo as a commercial hub and transit point. The international community has also called for restraint, urging dialogue to prevent further destabilization.\n\nThe federal government has not officially commented on the troop movements, while Puntland officials have cited the need to protect their territory. Analysts suggest that the tensions reflect deeper disagreements over constitutional reforms, resource sharing, and the distribution of political power.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-federal-government-puntland-galkayo-tensions-potential-conflict/",
